course: dictionary
lesson: 5737
title: Lottery: a game or a matter of luck
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lottery is a game where people buy numbered tickets, and winners are picked by chance. «The lottery is a game where people buy a ticket and pick numbers.» The important idea is chance: nobody can choose who wins, so luck decides.
explain: The word has a second meaning. Anything decided mostly by luck can be called a lottery. «Getting a place at that school is a lottery.» No one is holding a real draw here, but the result depends on luck, not just on effort.
explain: Use context clues to check which meaning fits. Ask: is the sentence about tickets and numbers, or about something that feels like luck? Tickets and numbers point to the game. Words like «a place at that school» point to the second meaning.
explain: Lotteries also show how small some chances are. Winning is very rare, so you can learn about chance from lotteries in maths. Children are too young to play, but you can still understand the word and what it tells you.
fact: A lottery is a game where people buy numbered tickets and winners are picked by chance.
fact: «Lottery» can also mean any situation that is decided by luck.
fact: The chance of the biggest prize is about one in 45 million, so almost everyone wins nothing.
fact: Useful phrases: win the lottery, lottery ticket, it's a lottery.
fact: A guess is not a fact: «they won the lottery» is a guess made up to explain things we can see.
exercise: «The lottery is a game where people buy a ticket and pick numbers.» What is a lottery? | A shop that sells bus tickets | *A game where winners are picked by chance | A test where you must read fast | A race between runners
exercise: «Getting a place at that school is a lottery.» What does lottery suggest here? | Hard work always wins | A shop sells school places | Everybody gets a place | *Luck decides the result
exercise: «The chance of the biggest prize is about one in 45 million.» What does this number tell you? | Everyone who plays wins | Winning is very easy | *Winning is very rare | Half the people win a prize
test: «A scratch card is a quick lottery ticket; the "almost won" feeling is designed to make people buy another.» What does almost won mean? | Won a big prize | Lost the ticket on the bus | *Nearly won, but did not | Won the most money of all
test: «A new car and builders in the kitchen are facts; «they won the lottery» is a guess made up to explain them.» Which part is the guess? | Both are facts | A new car | *They won the lottery | Builders in the kitchen
exam: Ella says «It's a lottery» about getting a place at school. What does she mean? | She has won money in a lottery | *The result is decided mostly by luck | It is a maths lesson about tickets | The school runs a real lottery machine
